From 36a2504ca961b99b4852688c9e1771263aa72613 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:22:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] subagent.roster: read the observation scope once per
 request, and the gateway name without scanning replicas
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 0.9 Hz Desktop sidebar poll `subagent.roster` cost 5-12 ms thread-CPU per call on the hub while K=6 conversations
had live children or background processes (0.59% of a core during the startup burst seen on serve 2785815: 476 calls,
3.6 s over 609 s, which alone failed the 1.0% whole-RPC P1 bucket for card J residual RPC t_01a1d86283da489b).

Five whys
1. Why is roster expensive only with live owners? With none it returns before projecting observations; with K owners
   it projects one execution observation per owner (child or process).
2. Why is each projection ~0.7-1 ms? Every one calls `local_scope()`, which re-reads `kanban_db_sync.sync_config()`
   (YAML config) and the hub's own gateway name. Attribution on the hub, K=6: `_roster_observations` 5.19 ms, of which
   `local_scope` 4.27 ms (`sync_config` 2.84, `gateway_names` 1.03).
3. Why does the gateway name cost so much? `_gateway_name` used `kanban_origin.gateway_names()[0]`. Its memo keys on
   hub.db and hub.db-wal stat signatures, and hermes-hub writes its WAL continuously, so the memo misses about once a
   second; each miss runs `SELECT DISTINCT gw FROM replica` (a covering-index scan of every replica row: 2.3 ms at the
   hub's row count) and then discards the known-gateway set.
4. Why per owner? The scope is process-wide within one request (config, hub.db, launch profile), but it was read
   inside the per-record helper `_subagent_observation` and inside `process_observations`.
5. Why not caught? The read-budget tests pin gateway/dispatcher ticks, not Desktop poll RPCs, and the roster tests
   used one owner, so neither the per-owner multiplier nor the replica scan was visible.

Fix (design doc + independent review deleg_e2143a25, changes adopted)
- `kanban_origin.gateway_name()`: exactly `gateway_names()[0]` without the replica scan. A still-valid
  `gateway_names` memo answers; otherwise it reads only the `meta` primary-key row and the mail config. Not memoized
  itself (a WAL-keyed memo would almost never hold). `_read_gateway_names(..., names=False)` shares the one reader, so
  every branch (meta, empty/absent meta, sqlite error, no hub.db, mail fallback) stays identical.
- `_gateway_name` uses it; `kanban.sync.gateway` still wins first.
- `subagent.roster` and `subagent.list` read `_observation_scope()` (local_scope) once per request and pass it down to
  `_subagent_observation` and `process_observations(scope=...)`; no request with zero rows reads it; every request
  still re-reads it (no cross-request cache), so a config/hub.db change is visible on the next poll. A scope failure
  still yields unscoped observations (gateway None), not dropped rows. Other callers keep the old default path.

Proof
- RED (production reverted, new tests only): 8 of the 10 new tests fail (local_scope read once per row;
  `gateway_name` missing / replica statement executed); the other 2 are preservation tests that pass on both. GREEN: all pass.
- 202 consumer test files importing the changed modules: 1783 passed, 0 failed. scripts/check --base origin/main OK.
- §5 scales, K=6, N=150, isolated fixtures with a hub.db write before every call (memo cannot hide the read),
  thread-CPU per roster call, median base -> fix:
    replicas 10k / sessions 5k   14.77 -> 3.89 ms
    replicas 40k / sessions 5k   19.33 -> 3.36 ms
    replicas 10k / sessions 23k  11.54 -> 3.34 ms
    replicas 40k / sessions 23k  18.91 -> 3.00 ms
    + 447 MB WAL on hub.db and state.db  20.44 -> 2.86 ms
  Base grows 1.64x from 10k->40k replicas; the fix is flat (<=1.0x, growth bound 1.5 met). Tasks are not an input
  of this path (no kanban.db read), so the tasks1k/5.8k/20k dimension does not apply.
